5. Consider Online or In-Person Requirements
Think about where the interpretation will take place.
If your meeting is online, an interpreter who can participate through video or audio communication may be the best option. For conferences, appointments, or face-to-face meetings, you may need an on-site interpreter.
Choosing the right format can make communication smoother and more convenient for everyone involved.
6. Review the Interpreter’s Profile
Before making a booking, review the available information about the interpreter. Look for details such as:
Languages spoken
Areas of expertise
Professional experience
Availability
Profile verification
Interpretation format
